+### 12. Name the revision by its release label
+
+An owner reads "Butlers v1.0.23" faster than a 40-digit object id. Polaris
+leads with the label and keeps the object id beneath it, because a tag can be
+moved and the object id cannot.
+
+- **Input.** The captured tag set is every ref under `refs/tags/`, each with
+  the commit it peels to, taken at the capture instant. It is a deterministic
+  evaluation input with its own identity. Ref names and object ids are all
+  that is taken; tag messages and signatures are never read into the model.
+- **Form.** The first applicable of four forms names the revision:
+  - not read, when the tag set was not captured;
+  - tagged, when a tag peels to the revision;
+  - described, when the nearest reaching tag is some commits behind;
+  - untagged, when no tag reaches the revision.
+
+  "Not read" is never shown as "untagged": an absence nobody looked for is
+  Unknown (VIS-2).
+- **Binding.** Claims, evaluation identities, links and comparisons bind to
+  the object id. A tag that an earlier held evaluation bound to another
+  commit is disclosed as moved, naming both commits.
+- **Read authority.** Reading the tag set and counting commits back to a tag
+  read Git refs and commit ancestry. The observer's registry entry does not
+  admit either today, so until a registry amendment does, every label takes
+  the not-read form.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+    T{"Tag set captured?"} -- "no" --> NR["Not read: short id,<br/>reason and route; never 'untagged'"]
+    T -- "yes" --> Z{"A tag peels to<br/>the revision?"}
+    Z -- "yes" --> TG["Tagged: Butlers v1.0.23"]
+    Z -- "no" --> A{"A tag reaches it<br/>from an ancestor?"}
+    A -- "yes" --> DS["Described: nearest tag<br/>plus N later commits"]
+    A -- "no" --> UT["Untagged: short id,<br/>no release tag reaches it"]
+```
+
 ## Risks / Trade-offs
 
 Each known risk has a fail-closed answer; the one residual risk the owner
